    "question": {
      ":question/id": "98e879a8-6411-51e9-aa1a-5cb1e0c51703",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2{y}^{′}=2x+y,\\,y(4)=2$\n- **II.** $5{y}^{″}=2x+y$\n- **III.** $4{y}^{′}=4x+y,\\,y(2)=2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $2{y}^{′}=2x+y$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=2.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $4{y}^{′}=4x+y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
