    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "66fd4357-6d3a-5ce1-af62-e46594dda1e6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $4{y}^{′}=5x+y,\\,y(3)=-4$\n- **II.** $2{y}^{″}=2x+y,\\,y(3)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=-2$\n- **III.** $4{y}^{″}=1x+y,\\,y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $4{y}^{′}=5x+y$ together with the initial condition $y(3)=-4.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2{y}^{″}=2x+y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(3)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=-2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $4{y}^{″}=1x+y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "a6f07278-faf8-555b-a4cf-4dfaacff204b",
