    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5e9c4afa-0b40-598c-a4f6-1834971b8cbc",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $5{y}^{′}=4x+y,\\,y(4)=3$\n- **II.** $5{y}^{″}=2x+y,\\,y(2)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1$\n- **III.** $4{y}^{″}=4x+y$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $5{y}^{′}=4x+y$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $5{y}^{″}=2x+y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=-1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "1c87091d-dcde-56b0-b9d3-7a2a62060130",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "4590de91-c82b-54f4-9017-713789bc1d4b",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "true"
