  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166815,
      "id": "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1",
      "title": "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6a56826a-9e4c-5963-abae-03d52e1eee7e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $3{y}^{′}=2x+y,\\,y(2)=-5$\n- **II.** $2{y}^{″}=1x+y,\\,y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$\n- **III.** $5{y}^{′}=3x+y$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}=2x+y$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=-5.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2{y}^{″}=1x+y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2f75d1e6-f089-5642-84d9-882b5eee194b",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
